    "created_at": "2020-04-15T11:36:53Z",
    "updated_at": "2020-04-15T11:37:37Z",
    "body": "I don't know what's expensive/blocking about `cleanupSome`. I think the issue is more that, `cleanupSome` is useful in cases where it's difficult to yield to the event loop and come back. One use case of that could be long-running computational code. (I was wondering if another potential use case could be short-running worklet code, but probably this one doesn't make sense.) It would be that other, surrounding code which effectively \"blocks\", not the call to `cleanupSome`. The question is, whether we want to encourage this coding style, of having code which doesn't yield to the event loop, by providing an API that's useful in the context of this style, and if so, in which contexts we want to permit it.",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
